Tabcloud+

Save, restore and track window sessions over time and across multiple computers.

Originally created by Connor Dunn, with additional work (window/session
tracking) by Max (Furtuna) Maxim. This repository is a fork that keeps the
Chrome extension running now that its Chrome Web Store listing has been
taken down.

Chrome extension in folder "chrome"
Firefox and Android app are not updated by this project.


Changelog (this fork)
======================

Manifest V3 + local sync
-------------------------
1) Migrated the Chrome extension from Manifest V2 to Manifest V3: the
   background page became a service worker, raw localStorage was replaced
   with chrome.storage.local, and browser_action became action.
2) Replaced the cloud sync backend. Saved windows used to sync through the
   original author's Google App Engine service (chrometabcloud.appspot.com);
   this fork no longer depends on it. Saved windows now sync through
   Chrome's own chrome.storage.sync instead, so there is no server to keep
   alive.
3) Added a one-time, opt-in importer (Options page) that pulls existing
   saved windows from the old chrometabcloud.appspot.com account, for
   anyone upgrading from a previous install.
4) Fixed a leftover ReferenceError (deleteWindowNames typo) in the "close
   window" confirmation flow.

See chrome/CHANGELOG for the extension's own version history.


What it does
============

TabCloud+ lets you save the tabs in a window and restore them later, on
this computer or another, and keeps a saved window "linked" to the local
window that opened it, so tabs you add or close afterwards stay in sync
with the saved copy the next time you hit save.

On top of the original TabCloud, this fork adds window/session tracking:
1) When you open an already-saved window, the newly opened local window
   automatically tracks it. Any tabs added or removed are reflected back
   into that same saved window next time you click "save".
2) When you save a brand-new local window, that window starts tracking the
   save it just created, so future edits followed by "save" update it in
   place instead of creating a duplicate.


How it works
============

- Local state (which local window tracks which saved window, and your
  settings) lives in chrome.storage.local, backing the extension's
  background service worker.
- Saved windows (the ones you can restore, delete, or drag to reorder)
  live in chrome.storage.sync. That means they sync automatically between
  any computers signed into the same Chrome profile with Sync turned on -
  no account and no login screen required.

Important: storage change from earlier versions
-------------------------------------------------
Earlier versions of TabCloud+ synced saved windows through a Google App
Engine service at chrometabcloud.appspot.com, owned by the original
author. This fork no longer uses it for normal operation - sync now
happens entirely through chrome.storage.sync, as described above.

If you have windows saved in that old cloud account:
1) Log in to the old account in a normal browser tab:
   https://chrometabcloud.appspot.com/login
2) Open the extension's Options page, go to "Import from the old TabCloud
   cloud account", and click "Import my old saved windows".
3) This is a one-time action. It asks for permission to reach that domain
   only for the import, and does not contact it again afterwards.

Limitations of chrome.storage.sync:
- About 100KB of storage in total, and about 8KB per saved window. If a
  single window is too large (a very large number of tabs) it will fail
  to save with a warning - remove a few tabs and try again.
- It only syncs across browsers signed into the same Google account with
  Chrome Sync enabled. It will not sync to Firefox, a different Chrome
  profile, or a browser with Sync turned off.
- There is no more web dashboard for viewing saved windows outside the
  extension (the old appspot.com site served that purpose).


A note from the fork maintainer
================================

I use and genuinely like this extension, and when it was recently taken
down from the Chrome Web Store I took the time to update it for my own
use - mainly to keep it working under Manifest V3 and to stop depending
on someone else's server that could disappear at any moment.
Nevertheless, feel free to use it as much as you would like.
